1
00:00:00,225 --> 00:00:02,625
You've probably heard that microwaves cook

2
00:00:02,625 --> 00:00:05,013
food from the inside out. It sounds

3
00:00:05,013 --> 00:00:07,275
logical, but this common kitchen myth is

4
00:00:07,275 --> 00:00:09,275
actually completely backwards.

5
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:12,725
In reality, microwave energy struggles to

6
00:00:12,725 --> 00:00:14,725
penetrate much further than a single

7
00:00:14,725 --> 00:00:17,400
centimeter into your leftover lasagna.

8
00:00:18,625 --> 00:00:20,488
In this video, we'll explore how a

9
00:00:20,488 --> 00:00:23,163
specialized vacuum tube turns direct

10
00:00:23,163 --> 00:00:25,600
current into invisible energy waves that

11
00:00:25,600 --> 00:00:27,550
get food molecules moving.

12
00:00:28,250 --> 00:00:30,387
We'll also find out why your plate stays

13
00:00:30,387 --> 00:00:33,025
relatively cool while your soup boils, and

14
00:00:33,025 --> 00:00:35,525
how the microwave door keeps you safe.

15
00:00:36,675 --> 00:00:39,788
A microwave oven heats food by exposing it

16
00:00:39,788 --> 00:00:42,025
to electromagnetic radiation within the

17
00:00:42,025 --> 00:00:44,025
microwave frequency band.

18
00:00:44,725 --> 00:00:46,625
The science behind this process is

19
00:00:46,625 --> 00:00:48,700
something called dielectric heating.

20
00:00:49,450 --> 00:00:51,838
Many substances in our food, especially

21
00:00:51,838 --> 00:00:54,000
water and fats, are made up of polar

22
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:56,300
molecules, which have an electric dipole

23
00:00:56,300 --> 00:00:57,225
structure.

24
00:00:57,950 --> 00:01:00,363
Think of a dipole molecule as a tiny

25
00:01:00,363 --> 00:01:02,988
microscopic magnet, with a slight positive

26
00:01:02,988 --> 00:01:05,237
charge on one end and a slight negative

27
00:01:05,237 --> 00:01:06,575
charge on the other.

28
00:01:07,275 --> 00:01:09,650
When an alternating electric field moves

29
00:01:09,650 --> 00:01:11,625
through the oven chamber, these polar

30
00:01:11,625 --> 00:01:14,275
molecules constantly try to turn and align

31
00:01:14,275 --> 00:01:16,475
themselves with the changing field.

32
00:01:17,225 --> 00:01:19,625
Interestingly, this process doesn't depend

33
00:01:19,625 --> 00:01:21,487
on hitting some special resonance

34
00:01:21,487 --> 00:01:24,300
frequency of water. Dielectric heating can

35
00:01:24,300 --> 00:01:27,275
happen across a wide range of frequencies.

36
00:01:27,950 --> 00:01:30,263
As the electric field keeps flipping back

37
00:01:30,263 --> 00:01:32,950
and forth, the molecules repeatedly change

38
00:01:32,950 --> 00:01:34,325
their orientation.

39
00:01:35,025 --> 00:01:37,362
As they twist and turn, they interact with

40
00:01:37,362 --> 00:01:39,513
neighboring molecules through electrical

41
00:01:39,513 --> 00:01:42,188
forces, transferring motion and energy

42
00:01:42,188 --> 00:01:43,475
throughout the food.

43
00:01:44,200 --> 00:01:46,162
And since temperature is essentially a

44
00:01:46,162 --> 00:01:48,550
measure of the average kinetic energy of

45
00:01:48,550 --> 00:01:51,138
atoms and molecules, all this movement

46
00:01:51,138 --> 00:01:53,025
causes the food to heat up.

47
00:01:53,700 --> 00:01:55,588
But to get billions of these tiny

48
00:01:55,588 --> 00:01:57,950
particles moving, the microwave first

49
00:01:57,950 --> 00:02:00,013
needs a way to generate those rapidly

50
00:02:00,013 --> 00:02:01,350
changing waves.

51
00:02:02,500 --> 00:02:04,400
That job falls to a clever little

52
00:02:04,400 --> 00:02:07,125
component called the cavity magnetron, an

53
00:02:07,125 --> 00:02:09,250
invention that originally came out of the

54
00:02:09,250 --> 00:02:10,600
United Kingdom.

55
00:02:11,325 --> 00:02:13,713
This British invention made it possible to

56
00:02:13,713 --> 00:02:15,913
generate electromagnetic waves with

57
00:02:15,913 --> 00:02:18,025
wavelengths short enough to heat water

58
00:02:18,025 --> 00:02:19,075
effectively.

59
00:02:19,875 --> 00:02:22,413
Inside the microwave, the magnetron works

60
00:02:22,413 --> 00:02:24,825
as an electronic oscillator, converting

61
00:02:24,825 --> 00:02:27,100
the direct-current electricity supplied to

62
00:02:27,100 --> 00:02:29,513
its vacuum tube into a high-frequency

63
00:02:29,513 --> 00:02:31,175
microwave signal.

64
00:02:31,875 --> 00:02:34,075
It does this by directing a stream of

65
00:02:34,075 --> 00:02:36,763
moving electrons past a ring of hollow

66
00:02:36,763 --> 00:02:39,450
cavity resonators, all under the influence

67
00:02:39,450 --> 00:02:41,600
of a strong magnetic field.

68
00:02:42,350 --> 00:02:44,375
As these electrons interact with the

69
00:02:44,375 --> 00:02:47,012
magnetic field and pass the cavities, they

70
00:02:47,012 --> 00:02:48,975
generate powerful, oscillating

71
00:02:48,975 --> 00:02:50,850
electromagnetic waves.

72
00:02:51,525 --> 00:02:53,975
But generating these waves is only half

73
00:02:53,975 --> 00:02:56,338
the job. The next challenge is guiding

74
00:02:56,338 --> 00:02:58,425
them safely out of the vacuum tube and

75
00:02:58,425 --> 00:03:00,000
into the cooking chamber.

76
00:03:01,150 --> 00:03:03,350
To keep the microwave radiation from

77
00:03:03,350 --> 00:03:05,500
escaping, the cooking compartment is

78
00:03:05,500 --> 00:03:08,075
designed to work like a Faraday cage.

79
00:03:08,875 --> 00:03:10,887
Even though the metal surfaces around the

80
00:03:10,887 --> 00:03:13,275
door don't need to touch continuously,

81
00:03:13,387 --> 00:03:15,787
specially designed choke connections along

82
00:03:15,787 --> 00:03:18,062
the edges effectively create the same

83
00:03:18,062 --> 00:03:21,050
shielding effect at microwave frequencies.

84
00:03:21,750 --> 00:03:24,137
The viewing window has its own safety

85
00:03:24,137 --> 00:03:26,875
feature too. Behind the clear outer panel

86
00:03:26,875 --> 00:03:29,050
sits a conductive metal mesh that helps

87
00:03:29,050 --> 00:03:30,650
contain the radiation.

88
00:03:31,375 --> 00:03:33,550
The holes in this mesh are much smaller

89
00:03:33,550 --> 00:03:35,650
than the wavelength of microwaves, so the

90
00:03:35,650 --> 00:03:38,200
radiation stays inside, while visible

91
00:03:38,200 --> 00:03:40,262
light can still pass through and let you

92
00:03:40,262 --> 00:03:41,525
see your food.

93
00:03:42,275 --> 00:03:44,675
Now, there's another important detail: the

94
00:03:44,675 --> 00:03:47,275
container you put inside the microwave.

95
00:03:48,075 --> 00:03:50,325
You need cookware that allows the waves to

96
00:03:50,325 --> 00:03:52,862
pass through. Metal dishes reflect the

97
00:03:52,862 --> 00:03:55,137
radiation away from the food, while

98
00:03:55,137 --> 00:03:57,412
high-permittivity materials absorb the

99
00:03:57,412 --> 00:03:59,675
energy themselves and can end up heating

100
00:03:59,675 --> 00:04:00,675
instead.

101
00:04:01,400 --> 00:04:03,550
When these waves hit suitable food,

102
00:04:03,662 --> 00:04:06,587
radiation at two point four five gigahertz

103
00:04:06,587 --> 00:04:08,737
typically penetrates only about one

104
00:04:08,737 --> 00:04:10,175
centimeter into it.

105
00:04:10,950 --> 00:04:13,025
In food with plenty of moisture and a

106
00:04:13,025 --> 00:04:15,787
fairly uniform composition, reasonably

107
00:04:15,787 --> 00:04:18,212
consistent heating happens only within the

108
00:04:18,212 --> 00:04:20,025
outer twenty-five to thirty-eight

109
00:04:20,025 --> 00:04:21,150
millimeters.

110
00:04:21,875 --> 00:04:24,162
There's another problem. When microwaves

111
00:04:24,162 --> 00:04:26,438
bounce around inside an empty cooking

112
00:04:26,438 --> 00:04:28,775
chamber, they create uneven patterns of

113
00:04:28,775 --> 00:04:31,225
energy, leaving some areas of your food

114
00:04:31,225 --> 00:04:33,587
scorching hot while others remain barely

115
00:04:33,587 --> 00:04:34,475
warm.

116
00:04:35,200 --> 00:04:37,337
That's why modern microwaves use a

117
00:04:37,337 --> 00:04:40,262
rotating turntable, a microwave stirrer

118
00:04:40,262 --> 00:04:42,725
fan, or even multiple magnetrons to

119
00:04:42,725 --> 00:04:45,200
distribute the energy more evenly.

120
00:04:46,425 --> 00:04:49,025
So, a microwave heats food by using an

121
00:04:49,025 --> 00:04:51,512
oscillating electric field to make polar

122
00:04:51,512 --> 00:04:54,062
molecules like water constantly twist and

123
00:04:54,062 --> 00:04:56,737
move, turning that molecular motion into

124
00:04:56,737 --> 00:04:57,475
heat.

125
00:04:58,200 --> 00:05:00,462
The cavity magnetron generates these

126
00:05:00,462 --> 00:05:02,613
waves, while the conductive mesh and

127
00:05:02,613 --> 00:05:04,875
specially designed door seals keep the

128
00:05:04,875 --> 00:05:07,175
radiation safely contained.

129
00:05:07,900 --> 00:05:10,275
And because microwaves only penetrate a

130
00:05:10,275 --> 00:05:12,963
relatively shallow layer and create uneven

131
00:05:12,963 --> 00:05:15,600
heating patterns, turntables and stirrers

132
00:05:15,600 --> 00:05:17,863
help make sure your food warms up more

133
00:05:17,863 --> 00:05:18,850
evenly.

134
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:22,450
Have you ever noticed cold spots in food

135
00:05:22,450 --> 00:05:24,512
heated without a turntable? What's your

136
00:05:24,512 --> 00:05:27,225
go-to trick for getting it heated evenly?
